   Kennedy Center � September 17, 2026

The judge required notice before a Kennedy Center demolition

   The September 17 order denied an emergency hearing but required written notice more than 30 days before the defendants implement a
   change to the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition of the main building. It did not decide whether
   demolition would be lawful.
   The storyJust the factsShow the work
   In this story
    1. What happened
    2. The catch
    3. Where this sits
    4. Who feels it
    5. What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong
    6. Claim checked against the record
    7. What happened next
    8. What we do not know yet
    9. The records

   >30 daysNotice before a covered change
   Sept. 23Court-ordered status report
   seven daysInitial temporary closure

   Three things to know
    1. Judge Christopher Cooper denied Representative Joyce Beatty's request for an emergency hearing on September 17. In the same
       minute order, he required written notice more than 30 days before the defendants implement a change to the project plans
       previously presented to the court, including demolition of the main building.^21
    2. The reproduced sentence does not say that every physical change is covered or state whether demolition would be lawful. It
       makes notice turn on changes to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.^21
    3. The executive director said the immediate closure was a seven-day safety measure unless extended. The defendants characterized
       it as separate from the board's renovation decision.^5^4

   The federal docket's September 17 minute order was not available as a court-hosted document in the public sources we could
   preserve. UPI reproduces the operative sentence below in full. Beatty's counsel quotes part of it and describes the requirement as
   30 days' advance warning; the surrounding motions, declarations, earlier opinions and board records are preserved separately.^21^18

   This story rests on filings in Beatty v. Trump and D.C. Preservation League v. Trump; the court's May 29 opinions; the board's
   August meeting record; the August 28 joint report; the September 16 scheduling order; federal statutes governing the Kennedy
   Center; the September 17 minute order as reproduced in full by UPI and quoted in part by Beatty's counsel; and the coverage records
   checked against those documents.

   The request and the ruling

The judge refused the hearing and added an advance-notice rule

   Beatty asked for an emergency hearing after the main building closed and after the board voted to close it for renovation. The
   defendants answered that the immediate closure was separate from the renovation decision. Executive Director Matt Floca described
   it as a temporary safety measure and declared that structural deterioration created acute risks to public safety. He said the
   initial closure would last seven days unless extended.^1^4^5

   The filing also put two public signals before Cooper. Trump had told reporters that without recognition for his administration's
   role in the renovation and fundraising, the building would close and end up being ripped down. The filing included a photograph of
   him reading a placard that appeared to say "Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED." ABC reported that the published images did not show the
   entire placard and that the visible word was cut off at "DEMOLIS." A Kennedy Center spokesperson separately said that nothing was
   being demolished.^3^19^26

   Cooper denied the hearing. He nevertheless ordered the defendants to give plaintiffs in both cases written notice before
   implementing a covered change.^21
   U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper�September 17, 2026�Minute order, as reproduced by UPI

     To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to
     provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the
     scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to
     any 'demolition' of the Center's main building.^21

   The order also required the administration to update Cooper by September 23 on the status of the temporary closure and emergency
   repairs. Beatty's counsel said Cooper also ordered discovery into the board's actions related to a potential demolition.^19^18

   The catch
    1. The period is more than 30 days.The operative sentence says notice must arrive more than 30 days before implementation. "30
       days" is a useful shorthand, but it drops the word that sets the boundary.^21
    2. The order follows the plans already put before the court.It does not say "any major physical changes" require notice. That
       phrase comes from the AP headline. The trigger in the reproduced order is a change to the scope of the project plans presented
       in the preservation case, with demolition named as an example.^20^21
    3. Notice is not a final ruling on demolition.The reproduced sentence imposes an advance-notice requirement. It neither approves
       nor permanently bars demolition.^21

   The earlier record

The court had relied on a no-demolition position

   In May, Cooper denied preliminary relief in the preservation case after considering sworn statements about the project. The opinion
   said the building's footprint and overall shape and size seemed unlikely to change permanently, and it described the anticipated
   open-space changes as relatively limited. The court said those findings were tentative and could change if the facts changed.^9

   On August 24, the Justice Department argued that without the administration's work and recognition, the Center would deteriorate
   until it was "required to be taken down." The filing said some people had proposed replacing it with a large outdoor amphitheater
   overlooking the Potomac River. That was a conditional litigation position, not an approved demolition project.^31

   The parties' August 28 joint report made the relevant promise explicit: the defendants would inform the plaintiffs and the court if
   the board's plans reversed the position that the building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.^7

   Congress appropriated about $257 million for capital repair, restoration, the maintenance backlog and security structures, with the
   money available through September 30, 2029.^30

   The August minutes say that keeping the main building partly open would leave the appropriation covering only about half of the
   work, with a $124 million shortfall after utilities. The minutes put the closure estimate at about $285 million.^13
   How the notice order aroseThe dated sequence in the court and board records.
   DateDecision
   Feb. 1Trump announces a two-year closure proposal. He says the main building would close for construction starting in July, subject
   to board approval.
   March 16The board votes on the closure proposal. The May opinion later enjoins implementation of this vote.
   May 29The court denies preliminary relief in the preservation case. The ruling relies on sworn representations that the main
   building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.
   Aug. 13The board votes 23 to 3 to close the main building for renovation. Programming would continue at the REACH and off site.
   Aug. 28The parties file a joint status report. The defendants again say the building will not be demolished and no new structure
   will be constructed.
   Sept. 15The board votes again to close the main building. Its chairman says renovation is to wait while an appeal proceeds.
   Sept. 16The main building closes temporarily. The executive director says the initial safety closure will last seven days unless
   extended.
   Sept. 17The judge denies an emergency hearing and orders advance notice. Covered project-scope changes require written notice more
   than 30 days before implementation.
   Sept. 23Status report due. The court requires a sworn declaration addressing the temporary closure and emergency repairs.

   What changes in practice

The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure

   The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope
   change. It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of the no-demolition position.^21^7

   The May opinion says several performance contracts would be affected, the Center had laid off at least 2 groups of staff including
   programming personnel, and 10 programming staff remained.^10

   The August board plan keeps the REACH open for educational and artistic programming, supports National Symphony Orchestra
   performances at off-site venues, and moves the Kennedy Center Honors and Mark Twain Prize off site while the main building is
   closed.^13

   In a public statement, the 8 preservation plaintiffs said the court should consider a receiver or replacement leadership if the
   Center's leaders would not protect the building. They also named a limited receiver, independent monitor or other neutral officer.
   They framed those measures as steps for the court to consider.^32

   On September 18, the Associated Press reported that thousands joined the protest outside and that some participants formed a human
   chain around the Center. The organizer invited artists, patrons, staff members, union members and volunteers.^33^34

   The defendants called the September 16 safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Beatty's reply disputed that account
   and argued that the temporary explanation was a pretext for an indefinite closure. Cooper denied the requested hearing.^4^6^21

   Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of
   a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels
   and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.^5

   Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit
   exterior pillars several times. That observation was sworn testimony from one witness, not a finding by the court.^2

   The board's August minutes record 26 votes cast: 23 in favor and 3 opposed. Another 2 trustees were present but did not vote, and
   several were absent. The resolution directed closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorized legal steps
   to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.^13

   Cross-source check

What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong

   The notice-order check uses 6 coverage records. 3 receive verdict cards below. ABC supplied the status-report detail and the
   placard limitation; CBS carried the Center's no-demolition response; and the earlier USA Today report carried Trump's ripped-down
   remark, which was checked against the filing that put it before the court. Another 2 AP reports document the next-day protest and
   Trump's response to a demolition question.^19^26^22^3^33^35

Associated Press via PBS NewsHour, September 17, 2026

   reported that the order required 30 days' notice before any major physical change and that all three recognition proposals were
   shot down.

   Mislabeled. The notice description is too broad: the order says more than 30 days and follows the project scope presented to the
   court.

   The recognition account is wrong: two proposals were enjoined, while the endowment inscription request was denied as unripe.

   ^20^11

UPI, September 17, 2026

   reproduced the operative sentence tying advance notice to project-scope changes, including demolition.

   Checks out against the full sentence UPI reproduces.

   Beatty's counsel quotes only part of it and describes the period as 30 days.

   ^21

USA Today, September 17, 2026

   reported that the order blocked immediate demolition rather than deciding whether demolition could ever occur.

   Checks out.

   The mechanism is advance notice; the order did not decide the legality of a future demolition plan.

   ^23

   The headline claim

Claim checked against the record

   The judge said Trump must give 30 days' notice before demolishing the Kennedy Center

   Directionally right, but incomplete. The order binds the defendants and requires written notice to the plaintiffs in both cases
   more than 30 days before implementing a change to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court, including
   demolition. It does not identify a demolition plan, decide whether one would be lawful or impose a general notice rule for every
   physical change.^21

   The next dated steps

The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks

   The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for
   written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and
   October 9 for the close of discovery.^15

   The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines
   for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.^18^21^15

   On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary
   judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves
   dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.^24^25

   The supporting papers say the complete Delta report reached trustees on August 31 and that the board ratified closure on September
   15. The defendants argued that phased closure would create a $300 million funding shortfall, compared with less than $30 million
   for full closure. These were the defendants' assertions. Floca's declaration identifies a JLL report and a Delta presentation as
   filed under seal.^28^27^29

   Later on September 18, thousands joined the Hands Around the Kennedy Center protest, and some formed a human chain around the
   building.^33

   Open questions

What we do not know yet

     * Whether the September 23 status report was filed, and whether the initial safety closure was extended. Searched: the admitted
       docket material and coverage through September 19; the report was not yet due.
     * Whether the defendants have developed a project-scope change that would trigger the notice order. The accessible public filings
       through September 19 do not identify an approved demolition plan. Floca's declaration identifies a JLL report and Delta
       presentation filed under seal; they were not available through the public storage route.
     * Trump's intent remained unresolved on September 18. The Associated Press reported that he did not directly respond when asked
       whether he planned to demolish the theater.^35
     * The court's minute order as a standalone, court-hosted document. Searched: the District Court docket, CourtListener and Justia;
       the docket routes were unavailable to the capture registry and the Justia mirror stopped before September 17. The page
       therefore labels the carriers of the quoted sentence.

