FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_0727.txt
Judge: Kennedy Center must submit plans for changes 30 days out - UPI.com

Sept. 17 (UPI) -- A judge ordered Thursday that the Trump administration must submit any plans to change the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in writing 30 days in advance of the work.
The decision came in response to Rep. Joyce Beatty, D-Ohio, an ex-officio Kennedy Center board member, who had requested a hearing after President Donald Trump and the board shut down the center Tuesday.
Kennedy Center Executive Director Matt Floca responded that the closure was only for seven days "unless extended" to address "acute risks to public safety" and was "separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations."
This case is part of an ongoing lawsuit filed by Beatty to keep Trump's name off of the building.
The president took over the Kennedy Center soon after he took office in 2025, filling the board with loyalists. They announced the center was closing Tuesday after a judge ruled for a second time that he couldn't put his name on the building.
Judge Christopher Cooper also ruled against a plan to add an inscription below the sign that says "renovated and restored by Donald J. Trump."
Cooper wrote in his Thursday decision that Beatty's request for a hearing was denied.
But he added another order.
"To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building," the ruling said.
Trump has threatened to demolish the building if he doesn't get to put his name on it, saying that it's in poor condition.
"The closing will take place immediately, however, the Renovation and Reconstruction, which is a very large and complex job, cannot begin until such time as the D.C. Circuit rules on the Board's approved name," he posted Tuesday on Truth Social. "If the ruling is a negative one, which it should not be, and is not overturned by the U.S. Supreme Court, the Reconstruction and the Renovation of the Kennedy Center will not take place."
On Wednesday night, a photo was taken through the window of Air Force One of Trump holding a poster that appeared to say, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISH." The image has circulated around social media causing alarm.
"Closing the Kennedy Center without Judge Cooper's approval appears to directly violate his order. This is an assault on the rule of law," said Norm Eisen, one of Beatty's lawyers.
"The board cannot simply ignore Judge Cooper's rulings whenever it suits them. In this country, no one -- including the president -- is above the law," he added.
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_0727.txt
FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6921.txt
Trump says Kennedy Center could be 'ripped down' without his overhaul

WASHINGTON — President Donald Trump said the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts will "end up being ripped down" without renovations that he's threatened not to carry out unless his name is added to the side of the building.
Trump, speaking to reporters on Sept. 16, defended his position that he be recognized with an honorary inscription below the main sign of the Kennedy Center ‒ a move the center's board approved but a federal judge this week blocked.
"I think that the Trump administration should certainly have recognition," Trump said. "Because, frankly, if we don't do that, it's going to close. It'll end up being ripped down. It's in very, very bad, bad shape, very dangerous shape."
The Kennedy Center's board, composed of mostly Trump loyalists with the president serving as chairman, voted on Sept. 15 to close the iconic institution immediately to undergo two-year renovations that Trump has vowed to help pay for.
Trump and Kennedy Center officials have argued the center's disrepair after years of neglect has turned into a safety threat to guests attending shows. Congress has approved $257 million for renovations. But the Justice Department says without Trump’s additional fundraising aiming for more than $100 million, the work can’t be completed.
"It's been badly maintained for 25 years," Trump said. "I'd like to be able to save but it's going to take a lot of subsidy into the future, and for us as an administration to want to do that, I think the Trump administration should be recognized."
After the board's vote to close the center, Trump warned he won't pursue the renovations unless U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper's decision keeping his name off the center's building is overturned on appeal.
Cooper ruled the Kennedy Center's plan to add an inscription on the building that says "renovated and restored by Donald J. Trump" cannot move forward. The judge said the inscription violates his previous order in May to remove Trump's name from the center's title and signage and violates the 1964 law that created the center to honor President Kennedy after his assassination.
The Justice Department has appealed the decision on Trump's honorary inscription.
Cooper is expected to later rule whether the temporarily closure of the Kennedy Center also violates his previous order. The judge, in the same May order, blocked the Kennedy Center board's vote in March to close for two years, citing a lack of adequate information before the rushed action.
Reach Joey Garrison on X @joeygarrison.
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6921.txt
FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6951.txt
Trump ordered to give 30-day notice for any Kennedy Center demolition

Judge orders Trump to give 30-day notice before any demolition of Kennedy Center
WASHINGTON − A federal judge effectively blocked President Donald Trump from taking immediate action to demolish the Kennedy Center, ordering on Sept. 17 that the center provide a 30-day notice to plaintiffs before any demolition or other major changes to the center's main building can begin.
It means the legality of any attempt to demolish the Kennedy Center ‒ which Trump has argued is necessary without substantial renovations ‒ would likely be subject to the judge's review during the 30-day period.
U.S. District Court Judge Christopher Cooper ordered the 30-day notice as he denied a request from Rep. Joyce Beatty ‒ the plaintiff suing Trump over the center ‒ for an emergency hearing. Beatty, a Democratic congresswoman from Ohio who serves on the board, is seeking the court's intervention to block the Kennedy Center board's vote this week to close the center for renovations.
Instead, the judge ordered the Trump-aligned Kennedy Center to submit a sworn declaration from a knowledgeable representative of the center who can attest to the emergency repairs that necessitated the closure. Justice Department attorneys argued in a court filing Sept. 17 that the closure is "temporary" to address emergency repairs, not to begin a two-year renovation project Trump has pushed.
Trump and Kennedy Center officials aligned with the president say the center's poor condition poses safety concerns for visitors that require significant renovations, which Trump has said he would help pay for.
But Trump wants recognition in return for his financial support. Trump has warned that he won't pursue renovations to the Kennedy Center unless a separate decision from Cooper this week keeping Trump's name off the center's building is overturned on appeal.
Cooper ruled the Kennedy Center's plan to add an inscription below the center's sign that says "renovated and restored by Donald J. Trump" cannot move forward. The judge said the inscription violates his previous order in May to remove Trump's name from the center's title and signage and violates the 1964 law that created the center to honor President Kennedy after his assassination.
Without renovations, Trump told reporters on Sept. 16 that the Kennedy Center will "end up being ripped down" because of its dilapidated state. The same day, Trump was pictured through a window on Air Force One reading a poster that says, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED."
Beatty's attorneys cited both examples in a court filing as evidence that Trump is actively considering the demolition of the Kennedy Center. They asked the judge to clarify that his order issued in May ‒ which blocked a previous attempt to close the Kennedy Center ‒ also prohibits its demolition.
"Closure is both a prelude to and the necessary consequence of demolition," Beatty's attorneys wrote. "But out of anabundance of caution, Plaintiff also respectfully requests the Court to make crystal clear that its order prohibits demolishing the structure."
Reach Joey Garrison on X @joeygarrison.
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6951.txt
FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17.txt
Kennedy Center says "nothing is being demolished" as Trump says it could be "ripped down" - CBS News

A federal judge on Thursday ordered the Trump administration to give 30 days' notice before any "demolition" occurs at the Kennedy Center, an order that came less than 24 hours after President Trump said that the performing arts center could be "ripped down," and photos showed him looking at a document that said "Kennedy Center Demolished."
Before Mr. Trump's comments, a spokesperson for the Kennedy Center insisted Wednesday that "nothing is being demolished" at the performing arts center.
U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper on Thursday issued an order requiring a detailed report from the Justice Department by next Wednesday on the current "temporary closure" of the center and emergency repairs described in court filings by the center's executive director, Matt Floca.
The Justice Department said in court documents Thursday that the duration of the closure will be for one week, "unless extended" to address safety hazards.
In a declaration submitted by the Justice Department prior to Cooper's ruling, Floca said that "acute risks to public safety resulting from continued structural deterioration ... necessitated a temporary closure of most public spaces in the main building." In an email to Kennedy Center staff that was also submitted to the court, Floca said that any extensions of the closure would be evaluated on a week-to-week basis.
"To be clear, this is not a decision to effectuate the Board's separate vote to close the building for renovation," Floca wrote in the email. "It is a distinct decision by management to address imminent life-safety risks pending the Court's ruling on that vote."
Cooper on Thursday also denied a request from Rep. Joyce Beatty, an ex officio board member who sued last year to halt Mr. Trump's changes to the Kennedy Center, for an emergency hearing on what she called the "unlawful closure of the Kennedy Center."
Beatty, in court documents filed Wednesday, argued, "Given the unexplained and unlawful closure, it is possible Defendants intend to undertake immediate steps in order to make public access practically impossible for the foreseeable future, including by demolishing public avenues of access to the building, and thereby evade the Court's order by fait accompli."
Beatty also noted in the filing that a witness said they saw a "forklift run into and hit the pillars on the building several times."
Fencing was also erected around the Kennedy Center on Wednesday.
Roma Davari, the Kennedy Center's vice president of public relations, told "Real America's Voice" Wednesday night that "nothing is being demolished" and that people should ignore "the media that is saying that because no one has ever said that coming from the institution."
Thursday's ruling marks the latest in the ongoing fight over Mr. Trump's vision for renovations to the performing arts center.
Cooper ruled Tuesday that Mr. Trump's name cannot be put on the building, following a second attempt by the center's board to add it, saying: "Defendants cannot install memorials for President Trump or anyone or anything else at the Kennedy Center without Congress's blessing. The board resolution bucks a federal court order and a statute Congress enacted."
Shortly afterward, the board, which is filled with allies of Mr. Trump's, voted to close the center for renovations, a source said. Congress last year appropriated more than $250 million for the restoration. Cooper, who has been overseeing the case, must approve the board's decision, but he has not yet determined whether the vote was lawful.
Mr. Trump on Wednesday night reiterated that if he were to raise money for the Kennedy Center's restoration, his administration should be recognized — or the site's future would be in question.
"So for the Trump administration, for me, to get involved and to take that on long term and to carry it, or to raise money to carry it, I think that the Trump administration should certainly have recognition," Mr. Trump said. "Because frankly, if we don't do that, it's going to close. It'll end up being ripped down."
On Air Force One Wednesday night, Mr. Trump could be seen looking at a document that said "Kennedy Center Demolished" as he was returning from a rally in North Carolina. The photo was entered as evidence in the emergency motion.
The Kennedy Center, which opened in 1971, is a national cultural center and is the official "living memorial" to President John F. Kennedy.
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17.txt
FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18.txt
Thousands join protest outside Kennedy Center | AP News

Thousands join protest outside Kennedy Center, expressing concern that Trump might tear it down
Thousands join protest outside Kennedy Center, expressing concern that Trump might tear it down
WASHINGTON (AP) — Thousands of people joined outside the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts Friday evening to protest President Donald Trump’s plans for the facility, lending their voices to a chorus of organizations and individuals who have been horrified that he might make good on his threat to close and tear down the storied institution.
The crowd streamed in from the nearest subway stop, crowding streets in the Foggy Bottom area of Washington and locking down traffic as they walked, many carrying signs, as they joined in “Hands Around the Kennedy Center.” At one point some held hands and formed a human chain around the center.
Chris Raleigh, co-founder of Hands Off the Arts, which set up the gathering, said in an interview that he hoped the event would show that people are willing to fight to keep Trump’s plans for the building at bay.
One of the most iconic buildings on the Potomac River waterfront, the Kennedy Center has been a focus of Trump’s remake of Washington since 2025, when he began installing favorites onto its governing board. Late last year, he had his named placed on the center. A judge ruled that Trump’s name was illegally added and ordered it removed.
The Kennedy Center board, which is aligned with the president, voted Tuesday to close the center indefinitely for repairs hours after U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper blocked their plans to return Trump’s name to the building. On Thursday, the judge ordered the Kennedy Center to provide 30 days’ notice before making any major physical changes to the building, including demolishing it.
Raleigh said he, like others, is concerned that Trump could take the step without notifying the court. “Look at the East Wing,” he said. “We got up one morning and there was a giant hole there.”
Raleigh said the Kennedy Center is more in the public eye than the East Wing was. He added he doesn’t believe the president fully understands why people are so upset “but that’s our job right? This may be the line that no one expected but this is where the line is going to be.”
A landmark of the performing arts has gone silent
Protesters talked about the importance of the center as the pinnacle of the nation’s performing arts world.
Bud Wilkinson, a local member of the arts community, set up a chalkboard with Trump’s name written on it hundreds of times. Protesters flocked to him to erase the names and replace them with hearts.
“The Kennedy Center is vital to the arts in Washington. We need it. We need this,” Wilkinson said. “This is not just about putting the president’s name on the Kennedy Center. It’s about censoring the performances and the programming. That’s what started all this. So that’s what the nitty-gritty really is about this. So I want to replace all of this with love. That’s why there’s a heart.”
Alina Baciu talked about the opportunities and relationships that students at the Duke Ellington School of the Arts have with the center and how those relationships might be impacted by what’s going on.
Her daughter had numerous opportunities to take advantage of all the center had to offer as a student at Duke Ellington, she said. “I mean, they borrowed the beautiful costumes and the beautiful dresses from the Washington National Opera for their performances at Duke Ellington,” she said. They also performed on the center’s Millennium Stage.
“All of those things have gone away because of this craziness. And it just hurts my heart,” Baciu said.
Laura Steinberg, a volunteer for the last 10 years, said the loss of performances has already been heart- wrenching. “This reminds me of that picture from Tiananmen Square, where that young man stood in front of the tank,” she said of the throngs present. “This was my visceral response to this. I’ll stand in front of the wrecking ball if it’s going to come to that. My guess is many of these people would come down because we saw what he did to the East Wing. Nobody thought he would actually do it. So I think the shock has mobilized people.”
Friday’s event followed a tumultuous week in federal courts in Washington where filings and counter-filings flooded the courts as the administration continues pushing for the president’s vanity projects. The administration has made substantive steps to begin work on a number of projects, despite the fact they are still being litigated. Such projects include Trump’s triumphal arch and the administration’s takeover of East Potomac Golf Links and the U.S. Institute of Peace.
Speaking from the Oval Office, Trump said he was willing to save the center, which he said was losing money. He did not address a question about demolishing it.
“We have a lot of people working very hard on it to save it,” he said. “I can save it easily, but why should we be doing that and then raise money for the rest of our lives to keep it afloat? Because that’s what you have to do, if we get no recognition. I think the Trump administration should be recognized as having saved the Kennedy Center.”
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18.txt
FILE data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18.txt
Trump defends putting his name on the Kennedy Center | AP News

Trump defends bid to put his name on the Kennedy Center
Trump defends bid to put his name on the Kennedy Center
President Donald Trump on Friday defended his push to put his name on the Kennedy Center, saying he deserves to be recognized for trying to save Washington’s premier performing arts venue after his takeover was blamed for triggering its financial distress.
Trump did not directly respond when a reporter asked whether he planned to demolish the theater, as he’s threatened to do, but said “the place is a dump” and he would “do a better job building it than anybody else.”
He said the Kennedy Center would require an ongoing subsidy to stay open and suggested it would be his burden to raise money to fill that gap.
“Why should I do everything and then for the rest of my life, have to go out and raise money?” Trump said.
He said only he could have secured the $257 million that Congress has approved for Kennedy Center repairs.
“I think the Trump administration should be recognized as having saved the Kennedy Center because nobody else could do it,” he added, repeating a comment he made days earlier while campaigning in North Carolina.
The Kennedy Center board, which is aligned with the president, voted Tuesday to close the center indefinitely for repairs hours after a judge blocked their plans to return Trump’s name to the building. On Thursday, the judge ordered the Kennedy Center to provide 30 days’ notice before making any major physical changes to the building, including demolishing it.
Trump on Friday called U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper “a very hostile judge.”
He spoke in the Oval Office during a health care event as his Health and Human Services Secretary, Robert F. Kennedy Jr., looked on. The venue was designated by Congress as a memorial for the secretary’s uncle, President John F. Kennedy.
“I should be very careful because Bobby is standing right behind me,” Trump said. “And I cherish that, too. I actually like the name of Kennedy and Trump, Trump and Kennedy, because it showed bipartisanship. It showed a Republican Party and a Democrat party working together.”
A wave of artists have canceled their Kennedy Center appearances in that past year after the Trump-loyal board added his name to the building’s facade, renaming it The Donald J. Trump and The John F. Kennedy Memorial Center for the Performing Arts.
Cooper ruled that Trump’s name was illegally added and ordered it removed.
END data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18.txt

