        “In considering a motion for summary judgment, judges must ask themselves not whether

they think the evidence unmistakably favors one side or the other but whether a fair-minded jury

could return a verdict for the plaintiff on the evidence presented.” Stoe v. Barr, 960 F.3d 627,

638 (D.C. Cir. 2020) (cleaned up). “And in viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to

the nonmoving party and drawing all reasonable inferences in [its] favor, the court’s role is not to

weigh the evidence and determine the truth of the matter but to determine whether there is a


