        Next, consider Thole v. U.S. Bank N.A., which concluded that beneficiaries of a defined-

benefit retirement plan lacked Article III standing to sue under the Employee Retirement Income

Security Act (“ERISA”) because they would be entitled to the same monthly benefit payments

whether they won or lost the lawsuit and thus had “no concrete stake” in the dispute. 590 U.S.

538, 541 (2020). The Defendants submit that Thole precludes Beatty’s suit because she, too, has

no stake in the dispute, as the harms stemming from renaming flow to the Kennedy Center and

not to her personally.

        Thole presents no such obstacle to Beatty’s suit; like Sprint, it supports her theory of

standing. The Thole plaintiffs were retirement plan beneficiaries who “possess[ed] no equitable

or property interest in the plan.” Id. at 538. Unlike the plaintiffs in Sprint, they had not been



