               1. Article III Standing

       Article III “standing is not dispensed in gross; rather, plaintiffs must demonstrate

standing for each claim that they press and for each form of relief that they seek.” TransUnion,

594 U.S. at 431. Here, as the government recognizes, see Defs.’ Cross-Mot. at 11 n.2, Beatty has

standing to challenge the Board’s decision to strip her of her right to vote on trust matters—

which, if she is correct on the merits, is a right that belongs to her personally. Being deprived of

the right to vote is a concrete and particularized injury, which is causally related to the

challenged bylaw amendments and would be redressed by an order enjoining the implementation

of the amendments to the extent they prevent Beatty from voting.

