       The Court appreciates that, in both the charitable and corporate spheres, board meetings

are often scripted affairs. Staff, management, and board committees have ideally laid the

groundwork for large-scale projects well before they are brought to a board vote. Objections and

concerns have typically been aired and resolved by the time such proposals reach the full board.

And board members naturally rely on findings, analysis, and recommendations developed by

experts and other qualified personnel. The Court should not be heard to suggest that trustees


